---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/nav';
---

<Base title="Why — The Courtyard Block" current="why">
  <header class="mx-auto max-w-[920px] px-6 pt-20 sm:px-12">
    <p class="mb-4 font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-terracotta">
      The obstacle course
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(40px,5.5vw,68px)] font-medium leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.015em]">
      Why does this take so much work?
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[65ch] text-[22px] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80">
      American cities already have courtyard housing. Making more of the shared, accessible, affordable places we want involves several connected decisions. Here are eight places to look—without pretending Europe has one housing system or one perfect building.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[920px] px-6 pt-14 sm:px-12">
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">01</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Which uses can share an address?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">Zoning can separate housing, commerce and other activities, or allow them together. The answer varies by parcel and jurisdiction. Berkeley distinguishes zoning approval from a building permit: permission for a use and envelope does not establish that the detailed design is safe or buildable. <a class="source-link" href="https://berkeleyca.gov/construction-development/permits-design-parameters/permit-types/zoning-permits">City permit guide ↗</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">02</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">What fits inside the allowed envelope?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">Height, setbacks, coverage and floor-area limits interact with the site. Mid-rise is one useful option, not a missing band everywhere in America. Test several envelopes and apartment layouts rather than assuming five to seven stories is always permitted, efficient or appropriate.</p>
      <figure class="mt-8">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 900 300" class="block h-auto w-full" role="img" aria-label="Zoning cartoon: allowed at 2-3 stories, allowed as a tower, missing in the 5-7 story band">
          <line x1="40" y1="250" x2="860" y2="250" stroke="#1c1915" stroke-width="2.5"></line>
          <rect x="40" y="130" width="820" height="66" fill="rgba(180,74,47,0.1)"></rect>
          <g fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.1)" stroke="#1c1915" stroke-width="2">
            <rect x="80" y="212" width="70" height="38"></rect>
            <rect x="180" y="196" width="70" height="54"></rect>
          </g>
          <g fill="none" stroke="#b44a2f" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5">
            <rect x="330" y="132" width="90" height="118"></rect>
            <rect x="450" y="132" width="90" height="118"></rect>
          </g>
          <rect x="640" y="40" width="64" height="210" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.1)" stroke="#1c1915" stroke-width="2"></rect>
          <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="13" letter-spacing="2">
            <text x="80" y="278" fill="#1c1915">ALLOWED: 2–3 STORIES</text>
            <text x="330" y="278" fill="#b44a2f">MISSING: THE 5–7 STORY STREET WALL</text>
            <text x="620" y="278" fill="#1c1915">ALLOWED: THE TOWER</text>
            <text x="46" y="122" fill="#b44a2f">THE BAND EUROPE BUILT ITS CITIES IN</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-ink/55">
          Fig. 07 — One possible comparison of height bands. Illustrative, not a map of US zoning.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </article>

    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">03</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Where do the vehicles go?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">Parking takes area and creates access and cost decisions. So do deliveries, mobility aids, bicycles and emergency vehicles. A court over parking can exist, but soil depth, structure and drainage differ from planting in the ground. Study actual demand and current rules rather than using a universal stall count or cost.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">04</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">What should the edges do?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">Setbacks may protect light, access or landscape; a street wall may support a more continuous frontage. Different conditions call for different arrangements. Pooling outdoor area into a useful court is one design option—not proof that every side yard is wasted space.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">05</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">How do people get out safely?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">The number, arrangement and protection of exits depend on the adopted code and the specific building. Galleries, balconies and required exit routes are different things. California’s State Fire Marshal publishes a single-exit/single-stairway study; a study is not an adopted permission. Evaluate safety with qualified designers and local officials. <a class="source-link" href="https://www.fire.ca.gov/about/resources/legislatively-mandated-reports">State Fire Marshal reports ↗</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">06</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Can everyone use the shared space?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">An accessible entrance is only the beginning. Consider the full trip: sidewalk, door, lift, apartment, laundry, garden and roof. Covered multifamily housing has federal accessibility obligations as well as applicable state and local requirements. A charming staircase cannot be the only invitation. <a class="source-link" href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/dfiles/FHEO/documents/TECHNICAL_OVERVIEW.pdf">HUD technical overview (PDF) ↗</a></p>
      <figure class="mt-8">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 900 260" class="block h-auto w-full" role="img" aria-label="Street width comparison: US arterial mostly lanes, European street with wide sidewalks and tram">
          <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="13" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1c1915">
            <text x="40" y="30">A US ARTERIAL, ~80 FT</text>
            <text x="40" y="150">A EUROPEAN STREET, ~80 FT</text>
          </g>
          <g>
            <rect x="40" y="44" width="60" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.12)"></rect>
            <rect x="100" y="44" width="90" height="40" fill="rgba(180,74,47,0.18)"></rect>
            <rect x="190" y="44" width="480" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.28)"></rect>
            <rect x="670" y="44" width="90" height="40" fill="rgba(180,74,47,0.18)"></rect>
            <rect x="760" y="44" width="60" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.12)"></rect>
          </g>
          <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="12" letter-spacing="1.5" fill="#1c1915">
            <text x="44" y="104">WALK 6'</text>
            <text x="104" y="104">PARK 9'</text>
            <text x="370" y="104" fill="#f6f1e8" transform="translate(0,-34)">FOUR TRAVEL LANES</text>
            <text x="674" y="104">PARK 9'</text>
            <text x="764" y="104">WALK 6'</text>
          </g>
          <g>
            <rect x="40" y="164" width="200" height="40" fill="rgba(63,92,69,0.3)"></rect>
            <rect x="240" y="164" width="170" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.28)"></rect>
            <rect x="410" y="164" width="170" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.16)"></rect>
            <rect x="580" y="164" width="240" height="40" fill="rgba(63,92,69,0.3)"></rect>
          </g>
          <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="12" letter-spacing="1.5" fill="#1c1915">
            <text x="44" y="224">SIDEWALK + TREES + CAFÉ, 20'</text>
            <text x="244" y="224">TRAM / BUS</text>
            <text x="414" y="224">ONE LANE + BIKES</text>
            <text x="584" y="224">SIDEWALK + TREES + MARKET, 24'</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-ink/55">
          Fig. 08 — Same right-of-way, different rooms. Widths illustrative.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">07</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Who carries the cost and the risk?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">Land, construction, debt, professional work, maintenance and reserves still need funding in a cooperative. Resident participation may change priorities; it does not make a project automatically cheaper. Land trusts and shared-equity arrangements also need enforceable agreements and ongoing stewardship. <a class="source-link" href="https://groundedsolutions.org/strengthening-neighborhoods/community-land-trusts/">CLT overview ↗</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-b border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">08</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">What happens after opening day?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">A courtyard creates a place where interaction can happen. It cannot guarantee friendship, inclusion or conflict resolution. Ask residents about noise, privacy, repairs, turnover, power and participation. Successful projects are places to learn from, not controlled experiments proving that a shape caused an outcome.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto mt-24 max-w-[1240px] px-6 sm:px-12">
    <a href={withBase('/berkeley/')} class="block bg-ink px-12 py-14 text-paper no-underline hover:text-paper">
      <p class="font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#e08b6d]">So: why not this lot?</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[clamp(26px,3.4vw,42px)] font-medium leading-[1.2] text-paper">
        The Berkeley proposal →
      </p>
    </a>
  </section>
</Base>
